diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — "pc2's addresses (200.1.1.0/24) have gone missing from router1's routing table because router2 stopped advertising them" — does not correspond to any observable fault in the current network state. Full investigation of every relevant layer shows the topology is operating correctly.

The BGP session between router1 (AS 1) and router2 (AS 2) over the 193.10.11.0/24 transit link is established and has been stable since lab startup with no session resets or route withdrawals logged. Router2 is correctly originating the 200.1.1.0/24 prefix via its `network 200.1.1.0/24` statement, which is present both on-disk (`/etc/frr/frr.conf`) and in the running configuration. Router1 has that prefix installed in its BGP RIB (`B>* 200.1.1.0/24 [20/0] via 193.10.11.2`) and forwarding table. Symmetrically, router2 holds the BGP-learned route to 195.11.14.0/24 (pc1's subnet). End-to-end data-plane verification — ping from pc1 to pc2 (200.1.1.2) — confirms 0% packet loss with sub-2 ms RTT.

The ticket's phrasing ("addresses have gone missing", "they stopped telling us where they are") is a textbook description of a BGP prefix withdrawal, but no such event has occurred. The network is healthy, the BGP advertisement chain is intact, and pc1 can reach pc2 right now. No corrective action is required; the ticket should be closed as a false premise.
=== END ===